## WebOSKeyMask is a plain enum without a fixed underlying type, so every OR of its flags (and 0) is in its range; the engine takes a set of flags as that enum type. The checker only accepts the listed enumerators.
clang-analyzer	src/platform/web_app_wayland.cc	optin.core.EnumCastOutOfRange	The value 'N' provided to the cast expression is not in the valid range of values for 'WebOSKeyMask'
clang-analyzer	src/tests/palm_system_blink_test.cc	optin.core.EnumCastOutOfRange	The value 'N' provided to the cast expression is not in the valid range of values for 'WebOSKeyMask'

## gcc -fanalyzer loses track of memory once a std::unique_ptr, std::string, std::vector or std::deque owns it - through libc++'s internals, a return slot, or a namespace-scope object freed at exit - and reports it as leaked where the owner frees it. ASan/LSan and valgrind find no leak in the unit tests.
gcc-O0	src/tests/mocks/web_app_window_factory_mock.cc	-Wanalyzer-malloc-leak	leak of ‘__ptr’ [CWE-N]
gcc-O0	src/tests/mocks/web_view_factory_mock.cc	-Wanalyzer-malloc-leak	leak of ‘__ptr’ [CWE-N]
gcc-O2	src/core/notification_service.h	-Wanalyzer-malloc-leak	leak of ‘<unknown>’ [CWE-N]
gcc-O2	src/platform/webengine/blink_web_view.cc	-Wanalyzer-malloc-leak	leak of ‘operator new(N)’ [CWE-N]
gcc-O2	src/tests/web_app_manager_config_test.cc	-Wanalyzer-malloc-leak	leak of ‘<unknown>’ [CWE-N]
gcc-O2	src/tests/web_app_manager_config_test.cc	-Wanalyzer-malloc-leak	leak of ‘<unknown>’ [CWE-N]
gcc-O2	src/webos/platform_module_factory_impl.cc	-Wanalyzer-malloc-leak	leak of ‘operator new(N)’ [CWE-N]
gcc-O2	src/webos/platform_module_factory_impl.cc	-Wanalyzer-malloc-leak	leak of ‘operator new(N)’ [CWE-N]

## gcc -fanalyzer reports the use of an uninitialised 8-byte stack region right at the entry of a function that returns a class by value (std::string, std::unique_ptr, std::vector, std::list, Json::Value): its event path is entry, region created on the stack, capacity 8 bytes, use - with no WAM statement in between. Every one of these reports has that shape (checked over all logs). Valgrind memcheck, which reports reads of uninitialised memory, finds none in the unit tests.
